Falsifies `device_tree_blob::verification::cells_refuses_a_value_wider_than_64_bits`.

The defect: `cells` drops its check that the value so far fits in 32 bits before it shifts the
next cell in. The loop still compiles and still reads one- and two-cell values correctly, which is
all most real trees declare; only a nonzero cell above the low two differs, and then the high cell
is shifted out of the u64 and the region is silently truncated to its low 64 bits, which is how
Linux's `of_read_number` reads it and what this crate refuses.

Expected to fail: the harness's `assert!(value[..low].iter().all(|&byte| byte == 0))` on the `Ok`
arm, for any value of three or more cells with a nonzero cell above the low two.

diff --git a/src/lib.rs b/src/lib.rs
--- a/src/lib.rs
+++ b/src/lib.rs
@@ -1198,9 +1198,6 @@
     let mut number = 0u64;
     let (whole, _) = value.as_chunks::<4>();
     for cell in whole {
-        if number >> 32 != 0 {
-            return Err(Error::BadValue);
-        }
         // An add, not an or: the shift leaves the low 32 bits zero, so the two agree, and an add
         // has no twin (`^` reads the same as `|` here) for a mutation run to report as alive.
         number = (number << 32) + u64::from(u32::from_be_bytes(*cell));
